# The shape a docs-drift task's final message must have — the ad-hoc face of a charter's
# `answer:`, passed with `verse task run --answer-contract`.
#
# It does three jobs at once, which is why it is worth having rather than trusting a prompt:
#
#   - it defines DONE. Without a contract the loop's only definition is the model declining to
#     call another tool, and a model that narrates its next step between actions looks exactly
#     like one that has finished. Observed: a run ended mid-investigation with "Let me focus
#     on:" as its result. A contract is checked when the model stops, the complaint is fed
#     back, and the run fails if the shape never arrives.
#   - it tells "nothing to do" from "something went wrong", which a runner cannot infer from a
#     clean worktree — the two look identical on disk.
#   - it makes the findings STRUCTURED, so the next hop reads a value rather than prose.
#
# A bare pattern is taken as the accept clause (see package answer).
#
# Deliberately SMALL, and carrying no free text. answer.Extract lifts a fenced block out of the
# final message, so the prose account lives outside the document and only the machine-readable
# part is governed. A `summary: !irtype string` field looks harmless and is a trap: a model
# writes `summary: nothing was wrong`, which is not valid tony (an unquoted literal after a
# key), and the contract fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the work. Govern what
# will be matched on; let the writing be writing.

status: !or [clean, fixed]
file: !irtype string
